{:actor/id "open-hax"
 :actor/kind :agent
 :actor/org "open-hax"
 :actor/label "Open-Hax"

 ;; :role/open-hax is a pure personality layer with no capabilities, so it is
 ;; composed with the functional role that actually grants tools. This mirrors
 ;; discord_automation's grant (:role/discord-user); widen it deliberately per
 ;; capability rather than by adding roles speculatively.
 :actor/roles [:role/open-hax :role/discord-user]

 :actor/policy
 {:principal :actor
  :notes
  "The DigitalOcean production automation actor. Owns the same tool credentials
   as the local PM2 Knoxx deployment's discord_automation actor.

   Credentials are NOT declared here. This repository is public, and tool
   credentials are actor-owned state in the policy DB — domain.actor.credentials
   deliberately refuses to read them from process env. They are written through
   PUT /api/admin/actors/open-hax/credentials/:provider, which requires
   org.user_policy.update in this org.

   Why this actor exists: tools called over MCP currently resolve no actor at
   all. agent-context is set only on the agent-spawn path, and
   domain.actor.credentials reads nothing else, so get-credential! throws
   'No current actor_id is available'. Discord and Bluesky tools are therefore
   non-functional over MCP. This actor is the production half of that fix; the
   application half is carding as knoxx-mcp-actor-ascription, which must carry
   an actorId through the OAuth code and token records.

   Shape note: katamorph.schema/ActorContract already declares :actor/accounts
   with :discord {:username :user-id} and :bluesky {:handle :did}. Knoxx cannot
   require katamorph yet — it builds a fork of it that lacks schema.cljs — so
   per-platform account identity is not expressed here. Add it when the cutover
   lands rather than inventing a local shape for it now."}}
